WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:02.440
- [Instructor] We're
told, you have a solid

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:05.330
that you know is mostly sodium chloride,

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:08.460
you suspect that it
might have or it may have

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.660
some sodium iodide, potassium chloride

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.290
or lithium chloride as well.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.110
When you analyze a sample
you see that it contains

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:20.150
73% chlorine by mass.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.870
Is the sample pure sodium chloride?

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:27.270
If not, what else does
it likely have in it?

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.930
So pause this video and see
if you can figure that out

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:33.440
and it will likely be useful
to have a period table handy

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.333
to do it.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.030
All right, so the way we're
going to approach it is

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:39.950
we're gonna think about
each of these compounds

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:43.630
and try to figure out the
percent chlorine by mass.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:45.820
And to do that, we have to think about

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.350
the molar masses of the
various constituent atoms

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.410
or the various constituent elements

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.210
that make up those compounds.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.270
And to figure out the
molar mass for each element

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.560
I just have to look at
its average atomic mass

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.600
and say, okay I'm going to have that many

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.410
grams per mole, if I
have a mole of that atom.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.140
Sodium,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:10.220
chlorine,

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.720
iodine,

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.150
potassium,

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.220
and lithium.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:19.243
And the sodium's average
atomic mass is 22.99,

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:25.750
chlorine is 35.45,

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.060
iodine

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:27.893
is

00:01:27.893 --> 00:01:31.860
126.90,

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.460
potassium is 39.10,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.460
and lithium is 6.94.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.400
And so now I can remove my
period table of elements.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.200
And so let's first think
about sodium chloride.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.130
So,

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.900
percent chlorine...

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:52.190
Actually let me make a little table here,

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.520
I think that will be the
most useful thing to do.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:59.400
So here I will put the various compounds.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.900
Compound.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.420
And here I will put the percent

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.670
Cl

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.160
by mass.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.000
By

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:07.970
mass.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.460
So first we can think
about sodium chloride

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:11.950
and I'll do all of these
in a different color

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.760
just to make things interesting.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.860
So sodium chloride.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.040
So chlorine's molar mass is 35.45

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.950
grams per mole.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:25.210
And then we need to divide
that by the molar mass

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.400
of the entire compound.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.410
So that's going to be
the molar mass of sodium

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.070
at 22.99 grams per mole.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.410
Plus the molar mass of chlorine again

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.430
at 35.45

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:37.910
grams per mole.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:41.480
So we have 35.45

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.220
divided by, open parentheses,

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.600
22.99

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.950
plus 35.45,

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:52.360
close the parentheses.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:53.870
Then I get it equal.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.150
So it looks like it's
a little bit over 60%.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.450
And that's actually
enough for us to go on,

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:03.610
because if this si approximately 61%

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.690
we see that's that a
very different than 73%.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.390
So already it's very clear
that to the first question,

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.400
is the sample pure sodium chloride?

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.540
No it's not, cause it has
a different percentage

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.410
of chlorine by mass than
pure sodium chloride would.

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:20.720
This would be what pure sodium
chloride would look like.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.740
So it must have been
mixed in with something

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:28.540
that has a higher percentage
of chlorine by mass.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.610
So let's look at the other candidates.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.710
So we can look at

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:35.200
sodium iodide.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.810
So sodium iodide.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:41.120
What's its percent chlorine
by mass going to be?

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.670
Well this has no chlorine by mass,

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:45.330
so this is zero.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:50.320
So if you had sodium iodide
mixed in with sodium chloride,

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.760
that would reduce the average.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.820
That would reduce the
percent chlorine by mass.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.170
It wouldn't increase it.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:01.710
So we already can rule out this character.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:03.020
Let's look at the next candidate.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:04.480
Potassium chloride.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:05.550
I'll write that right over here.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:08.700
Potassium chloride.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.100
And so its percent chlorine would be

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.230
the molar mass of chlorine, 35.45

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:18.880
divided by the molar mass
of the entire compound.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.220
And so that would be the molar
mass of potassium, 39.10.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:27.440
Plus the molar mass of chlorine, 35.45.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.800
And we could try to calculate this,

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.330
but even when you look at
the numbers you can see that

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:35.590
the denominator here, this
denominator right over here

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:38.587
is bigger than what we
had for sodium chloride.

00:04:38.587 --> 00:04:39.970
And we have the same numerator.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:43.130
So if the denominator is bigger,
that means we're going to

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.600
get a lower value than 61%.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.873
This is less than 61%.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.300
And since this has a lower
percent chlorine by mass,

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:57.170
if it was mixed in, it
would average down from 61%.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:00.590
It wouldn't go up to 73%, so we can rule

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:02.050
that one out as well.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.040
And now let's look at this last candidate

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.980
and I'm feeling good
about it because something

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:07.190
got mixed in.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.320
So let's look at lithium,

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.410
lithium chloride.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.880
What is its percent chlorine by mass?

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:17.510
Well it's going to be the
molar mass of chlorine, 35.45.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:20.140
Divided by the molar mass
of the entire compound,

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:22.020
and I'll just write chlorine's molar mass.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.350
And then I'm gonna add that
to the molar mass of lithium

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:28.630
which is actually quite
low, 6.94 grams per mole.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:30.730
So once again, when we
compare to sodium chloride,

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.650
we have the same numerator.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:34.500
We have the same numerator
but we clearly have

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:35.780
a smaller denominator.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:37.980
This value is smaller than this value

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:39.610
and the other number is the same.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:42.150
So this has a smaller denominator,

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.460
which means that the whole
value is going to be larger.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:49.460
This is going to be greater than 61%.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:52.190
We take 35.45,

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:54.483
divided by, open parentheses,

00:05:54.483 --> 00:05:57.220
6.94

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:00.380
plus 35.45,

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:01.600
close parentheses,

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.160
is equal to

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.560
it's roughly 84% chlorine by mass.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:07.770
So this thing

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:11.840
is approximately 84% chlorine by mass.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.490
So that does the trick.

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:16.020
If you had some lithium chloride mixed in

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:17.580
with your sodium chloride,

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.680
it could increase or it would increase

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.560
the percent chlorine by mass above 61%.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:26.750
And actually based on these values,

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:28.390
based on the 61%,

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:29.770
the 84%

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:31.230
and the 73%,

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:32.400
you could actually figure out

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.560
what percent is your
sample of sodium chloride

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.760
and lithium chloride if you assume

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.560
those are the only two things in it.

